contract.

00:05:33.319 --> 00:05:38.517
In fact, option price is high to probability map and that is how this part of the market

00:05:38.517 --> 00:05:39.037
works.

00:05:39.417 --> 00:05:44.397
A call or a put is a claim on a slice of where a price might land, so a whole strip

00:05:44.397 --> 00:05:47.397
of them sketches what the market is charging for each range.

00:05:47.957 --> 00:05:50.697
The probabilities you back out are called risk neutral.

00:05:51.277 --> 00:05:53.957
The phrase does not describe a trader who feels nothing.

00:05:54.417 --> 00:05:59.337
A number you would use for planning can sit somewhere else because people pay to dodge bad

00:05:59.337 --> 00:06:02.697
states and will take a lower expected payoff to get rid of them.

00:06:02.697 --> 00:06:08.457
risk, neutral math folds that payment into the probability. A fat chance of a crash in

00:06:08.457 --> 00:06:12.917
that measure can mean traders think a crash is likely, or that they hate crashes enough

00:06:12.917 --> 00:06:19.297
to bid the insurance up, or both. One option price will not tell you which. A 2011 paper

00:06:19.297 --> 00:06:22.197
in the review of finance starts from a cousin of that idea.

00:06:22.777 --> 00:06:28.237
Option implied volatility was already a strong predictor of later realized volatility. The

00:06:28.237 --> 00:06:32.957
The authors also find that option-implied volatility and skewness predict later realized

00:06:32.957 --> 00:06:35.437
beta, how a stock moves with the market.

00:06:36.097 --> 00:06:40.357
Under assumptions they lay out, you can build a beta from option-implied return moments

00:06:40.357 --> 00:06:45.337
using stock options and index options, and you can do it from a single day's option data.

00:06:45.917 --> 00:06:51.217
A historical beta needs a run of past returns, so a break this morning is invisible to it until

00:06:51.217 --> 00:06:52.297
the history catches up.

00:06:52.777 --> 00:06:56.897
The option version can see that morning because the option surface is the only input.

00:06:56.897 --> 00:07:02.717
A 2023 paper in the Journal of Financial and Quantitative Analysis built a model-free

00:07:02.717 --> 00:07:08.537
joint-risk, neutral distribution for the nine industry sectors inside the S&amp;P 500, kept

00:07:08.537 --> 00:07:11.877
consistent with options on those sectors and on their weighted index.

00:07:12.757 --> 00:07:16.377
Dependence in that surface was highly asymmetric, and it moved through time.

00:07:16.997 --> 00:07:21.537
Split the correlation into a down-market case and an up-market case and the risk premium

00:07:21.537 --> 00:07:24.197
on the down-correlation comes out strongly negative.

00:07:24.197 --> 00:07:27.537
positive, while the premium on the up-correlation comes out positive.

00:07:27.997 --> 00:07:32.577
The author's reading is that investors dislike losing diversification when markets fall,

00:07:33.117 --> 00:07:36.097
and that they actually prefer high-correlation when markets rally.

00:07:36.557 --> 00:07:40.997
That is a price for a preference, not a coin flip on whether two sectors travel together.

00:07:41.577 --> 00:07:47.997
A 2018 comparison on archive ran 15 forecasting schemes across more than 21 years and graded

00:07:47.997 --> 00:07:51.997
the densities on consistency, local accuracy, and forecast error.

00:07:51.997 --> 00:07:57.717
On a composite they call the Integrated Forecast Score, risk-neutral densities beat historical

00:07:57.717 --> 00:07:59.937
base predictions for information content.

00:08:00.657 --> 00:08:05.157
Variance Gamma produced the highest out-of-sample likelihood of the prices that actually printed,

00:08:05.677 --> 00:08:07.077
and the lowest predictive errors.

00:08:07.637 --> 00:08:12.917
An ArchSile method, GJRFHS, was the most consistent across the whole density.

00:08:13.497 --> 00:08:18.717
Lognormal densities, the Heston model, and the Breeden-Litzenberger formula, came out biased

00:08:18.717 --> 00:08:20.357
and failed statistical tests.

00:08:20.357 --> 00:08:25.577
There is no one implied distribution. Change the extraction and you change the forecast,

00:08:26.137 --> 00:08:31.137
and some familiar recipes do not survive the tests. Inside the day, jumps dominate

00:08:31.137 --> 00:08:36.897
the tale. A 2020 paper in Management Science looked at inter-horizon value at risk in

00:08:36.897 --> 00:08:42.057
a jump diffusion setting and offered a displaced mixed-exponential model that can closely mimic

00:08:42.057 --> 00:08:48.317
a wide family of levy processes. On the S&amp;P 100 and on American options, once jump

00:08:48.317 --> 00:08:53.357
and diffusion were pulled apart in a consistent way, jumps accounted for about 90% of that

00:08:53.357 --> 00:08:58.677
inter-horizon risk on average. Option implied estimates moved with the market far more than

00:08:58.677 --> 00:09:04.657
historical estimates, and the historical ones did poorly in back tests. Read a risk-neutral number

00:09:04.657 --> 00:09:10.257
as what will happen, and you are reading a price that already mixes belief, a dislike of bad states,

00:09:10.537 --> 00:09:16.297
and a large jump piece inside the day. An event contract pays a fixed sum if a named event happens,

00:09:16.297 --> 00:09:21.477
So, its price sits closer to a probability of that named event under whatever mix of belief

00:09:21.477 --> 00:09:23.017
and preference the traders brought.

00:09:23.657 --> 00:09:27.957
The option surface is answering where a price lands and how the tails are charged for.

00:09:28.377 --> 00:09:33.257
These papers say that risk-neutral version often tells you more about later prices than a

00:09:33.257 --> 00:09:38.157
density built only from the past and still is not a forecast with the price of risk taken

00:09:38.157 --> 00:09:38.417
out.

